  "grammar": {
    "title": "5A: Past tenses - simple, continuous, perfect",
    "explanation": "**Past simple** is for finished actions in the past. *I cooked a delicious soup yesterday.* The action finished yesterday.\n\n**Past continuous** is for an action in progress at a past time. *I was cooking soup at seven o'clock.* At that time, the action was still happening.\n\nWe often use them together. *I was cooking soup when my friend called.* The long action **was cooking** was interrupted by the short action **called**.\n\n**Past perfect** (**had** + past participle) is for the earlier of two past actions. *When I arrived, they had eaten the sweet dessert.* They ate it before I arrived.",
    "examples": [
      "*I *cooked* a delicious soup yesterday.* (finished action)",
      "*She *was cooking* in the kitchen at six o'clock.* (action in progress)",
      "*The bread *was* in the oven when I *got* home.* (long action + short action)",
      "*They *had eaten* the sweet dessert before we arrived.* (earlier action)",
      "*I *did not like* spicy food when I was a child.* (finished past)",
      "*He *was grilling* fish while she *made* fresh bread.* (two actions at the same time)"
    ],
    "practice": [
      {
        "q": "What is the difference between 'I cooked' and 'I was cooking'?",
        "a": "'I cooked' means the action finished. 'I was cooking' means it was in progress at that time."
      },
      {
        "q": "When do we use the past perfect?",
        "a": "For the earlier of two past actions: 'They had left when I arrived.'"
      },
      {
        "q": "Why is 'I was wanting' wrong?",
        "a": "'Want' is not an action verb. Use the past simple: 'I wanted to try spicy food.'"
      }
    ],
